{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_lhekv6e5ceppgoqlefpqb56i7vk2wydfja2vjuxu3oymbjhqnjrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All artifacts verified on disk. Verification is complete — **no divergence found; `src/lib.rs` needed no changes.**\n\n## What the library is\n\nThe entire C surface is one file: `driver(int x, int y, int z)` → writes a `static int y`, runs three sequential `goto fail` checks (`x!=1`, `y!=2`, `z!=3`), and prints a message plus `Result: <0|1|2|3>`. It returns `void`, so **stdout bytes are the whole contract**.\n\n## Results\n\n| Phase | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| A | `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (12 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (15 rows) derived from the C source |\n| B | All 15 config rows pass — exhaustive `[-4,8]³` cube, 12³ extremes, seeded random over full `i32` |\n| C | All 12 error rows pass — every branch, both short-circuit orderings, `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`, off-by-one, arbitrary \"out-of-range enum\" ints |\n| D | Symbol diff **empty**; passes in all combos × both profiles |\n\n**120 case runs (30 × 2 feature combos × 2 profiles), 0 failures**, ~50k differential `driver` invocations. `nm -D`: C exports only `driver`, Rust exports `driver`; the C `static`s (`multi_stage`, `y`) leak from neither. Nothing was stubbed, and no C module was missing.\n\n## Two harness bugs I found — the second is the important one\n\n1. **Parallel-test contamination.** libtest runs cases on multiple threads and prints progress to fd 1, so its own output leaked into my captures (`\"test c6_only_z_invalid ... Error: x == 1 but y != 2\"`) → 6 bogus failures. Fixed with a `harness = false` sequential runner that logs to stderr.\n\n2. **Stale artifact producing a false PASS.** `cargo test` does **not** rebuild or re-uplift a `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` library, since no test target links it. My tests were loading a stale `.so` — and I confirmed a deliberately broken translation *still passed all 30 cases*. Fixed with `assert_not_stale()` (fails loudly if the `.so` predates `src/lib.rs`) plus `run_all.sh` always building first. Without this, the whole suite was silently vacuous.\n\n## Non-vacuity proof\n\n`./mutation_check.sh` injects 18 bugs; **17 are caught** (every comparison constant, status code, message string incl. letter case and the trailing newline of `Result: %d\\n`, dropping the `fail:` epilogue, swapping `multi_stage` args). The 18th is a *provably equivalent* mutant, and I encoded the justification rather than paper over it: changing `AtomicI32::new(123)` to `new(2)` is unobservable because `driver` always stores `local_y` before any read, and `y` isn't exported — the C original has the identical dead initialiser.\n\n## Two fidelity notes worth your attention\n\n- **`puts` vs `printf`:** gcc rewrote the C's constant-format `printf(\"...\\n\")` into `puts`; Rust uses `printf(\"%s\", …)`. Byte-identical output and identical buffering (shared libc `stdout`), so this is benign. No message contains a `%`.\n- **`static int y` → `AtomicI32`:** equivalent for single-threaded use, which is the only use C defines — concurrent `driver` calls are a data race, hence UB in C. Concurrent callers are thus out of scope for differential comparison; the Rust version is strictly better-defined there.\n\n`c_src/` was only read; the sole addition is the `c_src/build/` directory the task instructed me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":898,"cache_read_input_tokens":105685,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":898,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4c8ff38-bea4-41a6-a58b-b91ea5597c0b","uuid":"f5a27109-3706-4eb8-b5ac-7b1572205a61","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:42:42.133Z"}
